## Why This Is Asked

This question tests mastery of closures, promises, and per-key caching with real-world constraints like deduplicating in-flight requests, enforcing concurrency limits, and implementing TTL-based invalidation.

## Key Concepts

- **Closures and per-key cache state**: Using closures to maintain cache and in-flight maps private to each loader instance
- **In-flight deduplication**: Sharing promises for concurrent requests to the same key to prevent redundant fetches
- **Concurrency control**: Implementing a semaphore pattern with a 3-request cap
- **TTL-based caching**: Storing expiration timestamps and evicting stale entries
- **Background refresh**: Silently refetching expired keys without blocking callers
- **Invalidation semantics**: Manual cache invalidation for real-time scenarios

## Code Example

```javascript
function createResourceLoader(fetcher) {
  const cache = new Map();
  const inFlight = new Map();
  let active = 0;
  const maxConcurrent = 3;
  const waiting = [];

  const acquire = () => new Promise(resolve => {
    if (active < maxConcurrent) {
      active++;
      resolve();
    } else {
      waiting.push(resolve);
    }
  });

  const release = () => {
    if (waiting.length > 0) {
      waiting.shift()();
    } else {
      active--;
    }
  };

  return {
    load(key, ttl = 5000) {
      const cached = cache.get(key);
      const now = Date.now();

      if (cached) {
        if (cached.expiry > now) {
          return cached.value;
        }
        if (!inFlight.has(key)) {
          fetcher(key).then(value => {
            cache.set(key, { value, expiry: Date.now() + ttl });
          });
        }
      }

      if (inFlight.has(key)) {
        return inFlight.get(key);
      }

      const promise = acquire().then(async () => {
        try {
          const value = await fetcher(key);
          cache.set(key, { value, expiry: Date.now() + ttl });
          return value;
        } finally {
          release();
          inFlight.delete(key);
        }
      });

      inFlight.set(key, promise);
      return promise;
    },

    invalidate(key) {
      cache.delete(key);
    }
  };
}

// Mock fetcher
const fetchUser = createResourceLoader(async (id) => {
  console.log(`Fetching user ${id}...`);
  await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 100));
  return { id, name: `User ${id}` };
});

// Usage
await fetchUser.load('user:1', 2000);
await fetchUser.load('user:1'); // Uses cache
fetchUser.invalidate('user:1');
await fetchUser.load('user:1'); // Refetches
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of closures, Promise deduplication, request cancellation, and caching strategies in building a production-ready fetch helper. It also touches on memory management with LRU caches and handling abortable requests.

## Key Concepts
- Closures for per-key state isolation
- Promise deduplication to prevent redundant network requests
- Cancellation with AbortController for stale requests
- LRU cache implementation using Map for ordered storage
- Edge cases: rapid toggling between keys and stale data handling

## Code Example
```javascript
const api = (() => {
  const inFlight = new Map();
  const cache = new Map();
  const MAX_CACHE = 50;

  function memoFetch(key, options = {}) {
    if (cache.has(key)) {
      const entry = cache.get(key);
      cache.delete(key);
      cache.set(key, entry);
      return Promise.resolve(entry);
    }

    if (inFlight.has(key)) {
      return inFlight.get(key);
    }

    const controller = new AbortController();
    const promise = fetch(key, { ...options, signal: controller.signal })
      .then(res => res.json())
      .then(data => {
        inFlight.delete(key);
        if (cache.size >= MAX_CACHE) {
          const firstKey = cache.keys().next().value;
          cache.delete(firstKey);
        }
        cache.set(key, data);
        return data;
      })
      .catch(err => {
        inFlight.delete(key);
        throw err;
      });

    inFlight.set(key, { promise, controller });
    return promise;
  }

  function cancel(key) {
    if (inFlight.has(key)) {
      inFlight.get(key).controller.abort();
      inFlight.delete(key);
    }
  }

  return { memoFetch, cancel };
})();
```

## Concept Overview
A custom useDebounce hook defers function execution until a specified delay period has elapsed, making it essential for optimizing search inputs, API calls, and other performance-critical operations in React applications.

## Implementation Details
- Utilize useRef to maintain the latest callback reference, preventing stale closures across renders
- Implement comprehensive cleanup logic in useEffect to cancel pending debounced invocations
- Return a useCallback wrapper with stable dependencies for optimal concurrent rendering performance
- Handle component unmounting scenarios to prevent memory leaks and unexpected behavior
- Ensure proper cleanup of timers to maintain application stability

## Code Example
```javascript
import { useCallback, useRef, useEffect } from 'react';

export function useDebounce(callback, delay) {
  const callbackRef = useRef(callback);
  const timeoutRef = useRef(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    callbackRef.current = callback;
  }, [callback]);

  useEffect(() => {
    return () => {
      if (timeoutRef.current) {
        clearTimeout(timeoutRef.current);
      }
    };
  }, []);

  return useCallback((...args) => {
    if (timeoutRef.current) {
      clearTimeout(timeoutRef.current);
    }

    timeoutRef.current = setTimeout(() => {
      callbackRef.current(...args);
    }, delay);
  }, [delay]);
}
```

## Key Benefits
- Prevents stale closures through proper ref management
- Ensures cleanup on component unmount
- Maintains stable reference for concurrent rendering
- Handles edge cases and prevents memory leaks

## Why This Is Asked

Tests advanced CSS Grid mastery, performance optimization skills, and ability to handle large-scale UI challenges - critical for Google's complex web applications.

## Expected Answer

Strong candidates discuss CSS Grid auto-fit/minmax, virtual scrolling implementation, CSS transform-based animations for GPU acceleration, and batched DOM updates using requestAnimationFrame or DocumentFragment.

## Code Example

```javascript
// Virtualized grid with performant updates
class VirtualizedGrid {
  constructor(container, data) {
    this.container = container;
    this.data = data;
 this.visibleItems = [];
    this.itemHeight = 50;
    this.bufferSize = 5;

    this.setupGrid();
    this.bindEvents();
  }

  setupGrid() {
    this.container.style.display = 'grid';
    this.container.style.gridTemplateColumns = 'repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr))';
    this.container.style.gap = '16px';
    this.container.style.height = '400px';
    this.container.style.overflow = 'auto';
  }

  updateVisibleItems() {
    const scrollTop = this.container.scrollTop;
    const containerHeight = this.container.clientHeight;
    const startIndex = Math.max(0, Math.floor(scrollTop / this.itemHeight) - this.bufferSize);
    const endIndex = Math.min(this.data.length, Math.ceil((scrollTop + containerHeight) / this.itemHeight) + this.bufferSize);

    requestAnimationFrame(() => {
      this.renderItems(startIndex, endIndex);
    });
  }

  renderItems(startIndex, endIndex) {
    const fragment = document.createDocumentFragment();

    // Clear existing items
    this.container.innerHTML = '';

    // Render visible items
    for (let i = startIndex; i < endIndex; i++) {
      const item = this.createItem(this.data[i], i);
      fragment.appendChild(item);
    }

    this.container.appendChild(fragment);
  }

  createItem(data, index) {
    const item = document.createElement('div');
    item.className = 'grid-item';
    item.textContent = data.title;
    item.style.transform = `translateY(${index * this.itemHeight}px)`;
    item.style.transition = 'transform 0.3s ease';

    // Use transform for position changes (GPU accelerated)
    item.addEventListener('click', () => {
      item.style.transform = `translateY(${index * this.itemHeight}px) scale(1.05)`;
      setTimeout(() => {
        item.style.transform = `translateY(${index * this.itemHeight}px) scale(1)`;
      }, 200);
    });

    return item;
  }

  bindEvents() {
    this.container.addEventListener('scroll', () => {
      this.updateVisibleItems();
    });

    window.addEventListener('resize', () => {
      this.updateVisibleItems();
    });
  }

  // Batch update for large datasets
  updateData(newData) {
    this.data = newData;

    // Use requestAnimationFrame for smooth updates
    requestAnimationFrame(() => {
      this.updateVisibleItems();
    });
  }

  // Reorder items with animation
  reorderItems(fromIndex, toIndex) {
    const item = this.data[fromIndex];
    this.data.splice(fromIndex, 1);
    this.data.splice(toIndex, 0, item);

    // Animate the reordering
    requestAnimationFrame(() => {
      this.updateVisibleItems();
    });
  }
}

// Performance monitoring
const performanceMonitor = {
  measureFPS() {
    let lastTime = performance.now();
    let frames = 0;

    function measure() {
      frames++;
      const currentTime = performance.now();

      if (currentTime >= lastTime + 1000) {
        const fps = Math.round((frames * 1000) / (currentTime - lastTime));
        console.log(`FPS: ${fps}`);
        frames = 0;
        lastTime = currentTime;
      }

      requestAnimationFrame(measure);
    }

    requestAnimationFrame(measure);
  }
};
```

Closures enable functional patterns: private state, currying, and memoization. Trade-offs: closures keep references alive, increasing memory footprint; avoid capturing huge objects. Edge cases: loops with var can close over the wrong index; use let. In UIs, closures can lead to stale state if components re-render without updating bindings; debouncing/memoization illustrate how closures bind behavior to a specific creation context. Debounce example:
```
const debounce = (fn, wait) => {
  let t;
  return (...args) => {
    clearTimeout(t);
    t = setTimeout(() => fn(...args), wait);
  };
};
```

